preConditionHook:
// THE BAR REACHES A FIBONACCI RETRACEMENT of the last `lookback` bars' range.
// The range is anchored on the window ENDING ONE BAR AGO, so the current bar
// cannot move the level it is being tested against. Long: the retracement is
// measured DOWN from the window high, windowHigh - fibRatio * (windowHigh -
// windowLow), and the bar's low has to trade down to it within toleranceTicks.
// Short: measured UP from the window low, and the bar's high has to reach it.
//
// WINDOW ANCHORING, NOT PIVOTS: two of the three merged sources anchor on swing
// pivots and one on highest/lowest windows; the window wins on mechanizability
// (it needs no pivot retention and no pivotStrength decision, which is itself an
// unmade choice at this level).
//
// ONE RULE, RATIO AS THE PARAMETER: 0.618, 0.5 and 0.62 are the three sources'
// levels and 0.618 is the default (two of three land there, and it is the ratio
// Brooks names first). Fibonacci EXTENSIONS -- 127%, 161.8% -- are the same
// arithmetic with fibRatio above 1, so there is no second rule.
//
// The window extremes mirror Extremes(), which is EL source: seeded at the
// window's first bar, scanning the rest, strict and toleranced (el_gt / el_lt).
//
// Touch convention as in TouchOfRecentExtreme: one-sided reach with a tick
// margin, never a two-sided band. Tick division-first into a local.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: lookback.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
windowHigh = high[1];
windowLow = low[1];
for (ii = 2; ii <= (int)lookback; ++ii) {
    if (el_gt(high[ii], windowHigh)) windowHigh = high[ii];
    if (el_lt(low[ii], windowLow)) windowLow = low[ii];
}
fibLong = windowHigh - fibRatio * (windowHigh - windowLow);
fibShort = windowLow + fibRatio * (windowHigh - windowLow);
